 R←Test_Select_011(stopFlag batchFlag);⎕TRAP;⎕IO;⎕ML;list
⍝ Exercise "ParseSelectKeyword", the keyword parser shared by both branches of `Select`.
⍝ Needs neither a user nor automation.
 ⎕TRAP←(999 'C' '. ⍝ Deliberate error')(0 'N')
 ⎕IO←1 ⋄ ⎕ML←1
 R←T._Failed

 list←'One' 'Two' 'Three' 'Four' 'Five'

 ⍝ The three keywords:
 →T.PassesIf((,'q') ⍬)≡list C.ParseSelectKeyword'q'
 →T.PassesIf((,'a') (1 2 3 4 5))≡list C.ParseSelectKeyword'a'
 →T.PassesIf('a~' (1 3 5))≡list C.ParseSelectKeyword'a~2 4'

 ⍝ "All but" with nothing or with non-existing items amounts to "all":
 →T.PassesIf('a~' (1 2 3 4 5))≡list C.ParseSelectKeyword'a~'
 →T.PassesIf('a~' (1 2 3 4 5))≡list C.ParseSelectKeyword'a~99'

 ⍝ "All but" may exclude every item; it's up to the caller to reject that:
 →T.PassesIf('a~' ⍬)≡list C.ParseSelectKeyword'a~1 2 3 4 5'

 ⍝ Anything else is not a keyword, and that includes invalid numbers after "a~":
 →T.PassesIf('' ⍬)≡list C.ParseSelectKeyword'a~x'
 →T.PassesIf('' ⍬)≡list C.ParseSelectKeyword'Two'
 →T.PassesIf('' ⍬)≡list C.ParseSelectKeyword'2'
 →T.PassesIf('' ⍬)≡list C.ParseSelectKeyword'*ee'
 →T.PassesIf('' ⍬)≡list C.ParseSelectKeyword''

 ⍝ The parser is case sensitive: folding the user's input is the caller's business:
 →T.PassesIf('' ⍬)≡list C.ParseSelectKeyword'Q'

 R←T._OK
⍝Done
